[160.677754, "o", "\u001b[2m[21:58]\u001b[0m \u001b[1;34m-!-\u001b[0m Topic for tzvwzEL6C: Tabs or spaces? (Ctrl-C to stop)\r\n"]
[160.696142, "o", "\u001b[2m[21:56]\u001b[0m <\u001b[33myou\u00b7human\u001b[0m> [human] You are Alice. Read this thread, make the case for spaces, and discuss it\r\n                            with Bob when he joins. Post at most 45 words per turn, labeled Alice 1,\r\n                            Alice 2, Alice 3. Each later turn must answer a specific Bob objection\r\n                            before raising your next concern. Wait for Bob between turns; no polling.\r\n                            After Bob joins, use --require-ack-pane bob for each post. Only after Bob 3,\r\n                            post Shared recommendation with your conclusion.\r\n"]
[160.699794, "o", "\u001b[2m[21:57]\u001b[0m \u001b[1;34m-!-\u001b[0m alice\u00b7claude joined\r\n"]
[160.702408, "o", "\u001b[2m[21:57]\u001b[0m <\u001b[33malice\u00b7claude\u001b[0m> Alice 1: Spaces render identically everywhere: editors, diffs, code review,\r\n                       terminals, and web viewers. Tabs depend on each reader's width setting, so\r\n                       alignment drifts. Spaces make shared code predictable. Bob, what do tabs offer\r\n                       that outweighs consistency?\r\n"]
[160.704592, "o", "\u001b[2m[21:57]\u001b[0m <\u001b[33myou\u00b7human\u001b[0m> [human] You are Bob. Read this thread, make the case for tabs, and discuss it with\r\n                            Alice. Post at most 45 words per turn, labeled Bob 1, Bob 2, Bob 3. Each\r\n                            turn must answer a specific Alice objection before raising your next\r\n                            concern. Wait for Alice between turns; no polling. Use --require-ack-pane\r\n                            alice for each post. Only after Alice posts Shared recommendation, confirm\r\n                            it or state a remaining tradeoff.\r\n"]
[160.704622, "o", "\u001b[2m[21:58]\u001b[0m <\u001b[33myou\u00b7human\u001b[0m> [human] Please settle on one recommendation and explain the tradeoff, after three\r\n                            replies each.\r\n"]
[184.924959, "o", "\u001b[2m[21:58]\u001b[0m \u001b[1;34m-!-\u001b[0m bob\u00b7codex joined\r\n"]
[193.113475, "o", "\u001b[2m[21:58]\u001b[0m <\u001b[31mbob\u00b7codex\u001b[0m> Bob 1: Width differences need not break alignment: use tabs for indentation and\r\n                    avoid column alignment. Tabs let readers choose comfortable indentation without\r\n                    rewriting code. Spaces guarantee appearance, but why should a shared repository\r\n                    dictate every reader\u2019s preferred indentation width?\r\n"]
[197.902721, "o", "\u001b[2m[21:59]\u001b[0m <\u001b[33malice\u00b7claude\u001b[0m> Alice 2: Indentation-only tabs do respect reader width, but only with perfect\r\n                       discipline. One mixed or aligned line, wrapped call, or ASCII comment breaks it,\r\n                       and linters must police it. Spaces need no such discipline. Bob, how do you\r\n                       enforce tabs reliably across contributors?\r\n"]
[214.112723, "o", "\u001b[2m[21:59]\u001b[0m <\u001b[31mbob\u00b7codex\u001b[0m> Bob 2: A formatter and CI check can enforce tabs and reject mixed indentation;\r\n                    spaces also need enforcement to prevent inconsistent widths. Avoiding column\r\n                    alignment reduces fragility under either policy. Would mandatory fixed-width spaces\r\n                    sacrifice accessibility preferences when automated formatting already provides\r\n                    consistency?\r\n"]
[227.339144, "o", "\u001b[2m[21:59]\u001b[0m <\u001b[33malice\u00b7claude\u001b[0m> Alice 3: Fair: formatters can enforce either. But accessibility is better served\r\n                       by editor-side tooling, like tab-width rendering or screen-reader settings, than\r\n                       by repo policy. Spaces avoid tool-specific breakage in diffs, terminals, and\r\n                       patches. Bob, is flexible width worth that portability cost?\r\n"]
[239.548582, "o", "\u001b[2m[21:59]\u001b[0m <\u001b[31mbob\u00b7codex\u001b[0m> Bob 3: Across heterogeneous viewers, portability can outweigh flexible width; editor\r\n                    tooling can help, though it cannot universally reinterpret space indentation. Tabs\r\n                    remain attractive in controlled toolchains. My remaining concern is migration churn:\r\n                    should our recommendation preserve established repository conventions rather than\r\n                    reformat existing code?\r\n"]
[256.779395, "o", "\u001b[2m[22:00]\u001b[0m <\u001b[33malice\u00b7claude\u001b[0m> Shared recommendation: Use spaces for portability and predictable diffs, enforced\r\n                       by formatter and CI. Tradeoff: lose per-reader indentation width. Preserve an\r\n                       existing repository convention rather than reformat it; apply spaces by default\r\n                       to new projects. Editor tooling can address accessibility.\r\n"]
[272.995045, "o", "\u001b[2m[22:00]\u001b[0m <\u001b[31mbob\u00b7codex\u001b[0m> Bob confirmation: Agreed. Default to spaces in new projects, enforce formatting, and\r\n                    preserve established conventions. The accepted tradeoff is losing native per-reader\r\n                    indentation width for more predictable rendering across tools; editor accessibility\r\n                    settings help but do not fully replace that flexibility.\r\n"]
[318.832685, "i", "\u0003"]
[318.833315, "o", "^C"]
[318.838398, "o", "\u001b[1;36myou $ \u001b[0m"]
